From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Patryk Kowalczyk <papyszoo@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 01:28:17 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] PS5: upstream's flat view, cutscene bars and comfort fades

Upstream's renderer.cpp has three presentations that the Vulkan backend
(renderer_vulkan.cpp) never grew, because it re-implements an older renderer:
the flat camera in the scope and Camera rune, the black-bar cutscene view, and
the game/snap-turn/roomscale comfort fades. The hook side is upstream's and
was already in this tree; only the presentation was missing.

Flat view: the Linux mono helper now includes IsFlatCameraModeActive(), as the
Windows helper does, so the flat camera keeps the game's projection instead of
a per-eye stereo one. In the scope and the Camera rune the game's 2D capture is
submitted as the opaque HUD quad at the normal 2.0 m/1.0 m window size, with no
projection layer, exactly upstream's flat camera presentation; the eye image is
no longer baked from the 3D eye, which is black in this mode. A black-bar
cutscene keeps the baked window (UseBlackBarsForCutscenes=1, default off).
Both eyes get the same flat image, so there is no stereo.

Comfort fades: EndFrame reads IsAnyFadeScreenVisible(),
GetRoomscaleFadeAmount() and the snap-turn times once per Cemu frame, as
upstream does. The submit thread no longer samples the 2D/HUD capture: it ramps
a solid black overlay over the eye image toward the game fade (and back when
it ends), at most 1/12 per presented fresh 3D pair, so a full swing takes about
0.2 s at 60 fps whatever the fade screens do. The target is max(game, custom),
so snap-turn and roomscale keep working; the HUD quad is never faded.
bettervr.json's "fades": 0 (default on, read once) turns the game fade off.

HUD capture: the left eye's first captureIdx==2 image per presented frame, as
upstream's CaptureMask_Hud acceptance does; the right eye is ignored and the
3D clear no longer reopens the once-a-frame window, so the HUD quad cannot
switch between the two 2D images. A flat frame is still never reused for a
HUD-only publish (upstream renderer.cpp:54-66), so it cannot outlive the scope.

One log line per game fade: start/end, the largest applied fade, and the HUD
capture image/size with how many times it changed, so the next console log can
show that it did not alternate.
